The coalesced sum of the two identity maps is the identity map of the coalesced sum.

The two approximable maps are equal because they relate the same neighbourhoods. In the forward direction the master clause uses membership of the input and the fact that every neighbourhood lies under the master. A proper left identity inclusion becomes an inclusion of false tags, with both memberships given by the false-tag clause of the coalesced sum, and the proper right inclusion is symmetric. In the backward direction a master output is the master clause. A left output whose input is the master would place the empty string in a bit embedding, a right-tagged input is an inclusion of opposite bits, and a left-tagged input unpacks the inclusion of bit embeddings, keeping properness. The right output is symmetric.
